---
import CourseLesson from "../../../layouts/CourseLesson.astro";
import Math from "../../../components/course/Math.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/course/Callout.astro";
import Quiz from "../../../components/course/Quiz.astro";
import ContingencyTable from "../../../components/course/widgets/ContingencyTable.astro";
---

<CourseLesson course="probability" lessonSlug="conditional-probability-and-independence">
  <h2>Conditioning shrinks the sample space</h2>
  <p>{" "}
    <Math tex="P(A \mid B)" /> is the probability of <Math tex="A" /> once you already know{" "}
    <Math tex="B" /> happened. Knowing <Math tex="B" /> throws away every outcome outside{" "}
    <Math tex="B" />, so <Math tex="B" /> becomes the new sample space and everything is rescaled to
    keep the total at 1:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(A \cap B)}{P(B)}, \qquad P(B) > 0" />
  <p>Rearranged, that is the <strong>multiplication rule</strong>, which is how you build joints:</p>
  <Math display tex="P(A \cap B) = P(A \mid B)\,P(B) = P(B \mid A)\,P(A)" />

  <Callout variant="warning" label="Read the conditioning bar carefully">
    <p>{" "}
      <Math tex="P(A \mid B)" /> and <Math tex="P(B \mid A)" /> are different numbers, and mixing
      them up is the single most common error in this whole subject. The denominator is always the
      event you are told happened. "Out of the students who do not like coconuts, how many like
      mangoes?" is <Math tex="P(M \mid C^c)" /> &mdash; the group you are standing in goes on the
      right.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Joint, marginal, conditional</h2>
  <p>
    With two yes/no attributes there are only four joint probabilities, and they must sum to 1. Put
    them in a 2&times;2 table and the row and column totals are the <strong>marginals</strong>:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>C</th><th>C<sup>c</sup></th><th>total</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>M</th><td>P(M &cap; C)</td><td>P(M &cap; C<sup>c</sup>)</td><td>P(M)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>M<sup>c</sup></th><td>P(M<sup>c</sup> &cap; C)</td><td>P(M<sup>c</sup> &cap; C<sup>c</sup>)</td><td>P(M<sup>c</sup>)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>total</th><td>P(C)</td><td>P(C<sup>c</sup>)</td><td>1</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every conditional is one cell divided by its row or column total. Fill the table and the problem
    is over &mdash; which is why the table, not the formula, is the practical tool.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example: mangoes and coconuts</h2>
  <p>
    In a class, 40% like mangoes and 46% like coconuts. Of the students who do{" "}
    <em>not</em> like mangoes, 40% do not like coconuts. So{" "}
    <Math tex="P(M) = 0.40" />, <Math tex="P(C) = 0.46" />, and{" "}
    <Math tex="P(C^c \mid M^c) = 0.40" />.
  </p>
  <p>Start with the one conditional you were given, and turn it into a joint:</p>
  <Math display tex="P(M^c \cap C^c) = P(C^c \mid M^c)\,P(M^c) = 0.40 \times 0.60 = 0.24" />
  <p>
    Now the table fills itself by subtraction along the margins. <Math tex="P(C^c) = 0.54" />, so{" "}
    <Math tex="P(M \cap C^c) = 0.54 - 0.24 = 0.30" />. Then{" "}
    <Math tex="P(M \cap C) = P(M) - P(M \cap C^c) = 0.40 - 0.30 = 0.10" />, and the last cell is{" "}
    <Math tex="0.46 - 0.10 = 0.36" />.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>C</th><th>C<sup>c</sup></th><th>total</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>M</th><td>0.10</td><td>0.30</td><td>0.40</td></tr>
        <tr><th>M<sup>c</sup></th><td>0.36</td><td>0.24</td><td>0.60</td></tr>
        <tr><th>total</th><td>0.46</td><td>0.54</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Likes both:</strong> <Math tex="P(M \cap C) = 0.10" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Independent?</strong> <Math tex="P(M)P(C) = 0.40 \times 0.46 = 0.184" />, which is not{" "}
      <Math tex="0.10" />. So mango and coconut preference are <em>dependent</em> &mdash; and
      negatively so, since liking one makes liking the other less likely than chance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mangoes among the coconut-averse:</strong>{" "}
      <Math tex="P(M \mid C^c) = \dfrac{0.30}{0.54} = \dfrac{5}{9} \approx 0.5556" />.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>Two events are <strong>independent</strong> when</p>
  <Math display tex="P(A \cap B) = P(A)\,P(B)" />
  <p>
    which is equivalent to <Math tex="P(A \mid B) = P(A)" /> whenever <Math tex="P(B) > 0" />:
    learning <Math tex="B" /> tells you nothing about <Math tex="A" />. Independence is a{" "}
    <em>numerical coincidence</em>, not something you can see in a Venn diagram &mdash; you have to
    check the product.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="warning" label="Independent is not the same as mutually exclusive">
    <p>
      Mutually exclusive events with non-zero probability are the <em>most</em> dependent events
      there are: if <Math tex="A" /> happened, <Math tex="B" /> definitely did not, so{" "}
      <Math tex="P(B \mid A) = 0 \ne P(B)" />. Disjointness is a statement about the sets;
      independence is a statement about the numbers.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    In the widget below, the mosaic on the right makes independence visible: the two column splits
    line up with the dashed <Math tex="P(B)" /> line exactly when the product rule holds. Start from
    the defaults (0.40, 0.46, 0.40) to reproduce the table above, then drag{" "}
    <Math tex="P(B^c \mid A^c)" /> until the steps flatten out.
  </p>

  <ContingencyTable />

  <Quiz
    question="P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.5, and P(A ∩ B) = 0.2. Which statement is true?"
    options={[
      "A and B are mutually exclusive",
      "A and B are independent",
      "A and B are dependent, since P(A | B) = 0.4",
      "Not enough information",
    ]}
    correctIndex={1}
    correctText="Correct: 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2 = P(A ∩ B), so they are independent. P(A | B) = 0.2/0.5 = 0.4 = P(A), which says the same thing a second way."
    incorrectText="Check the product: P(A)·P(B) = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2, which is exactly P(A ∩ B). That is the definition of independence — and P(A | B) = 0.4 = P(A) confirms it."
  />
</CourseLesson>
